course: dictionary
lesson: 5303
title: Proper nouns: names of places
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A proper noun is the name of one special person, place or thing. It always starts with a capital letter, even in the middle of a sentence. «Kansas» is a proper noun: it names one particular place, a state in the middle of the United States.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «A girl in Kansas hears in a video that a drop of bleach in water cures colds.» The words "in Kansas" tell you Kansas is a place where the girl lives. If you can put "the state of" in front of a word and it still makes sense, it is probably a place name.
explain: Some names also have a special meaning in phrases. «We're not in Kansas anymore» means things are strange and new here. You can work that out from the words around it, the same way you would with any new word.
fact: A proper noun is the name of one special person, place or thing, and it starts with a capital letter.
fact: «Kansas» is a state in the middle of the United States, famous for flat prairies, wheat farms and tornadoes.
fact: The capital of Kansas is Topeka.
fact: In «The Wizard of Oz», Dorothy comes from Kansas and is carried away by a tornado.
fact: «Kansas City» is the name of two cities next to each other: one in Kansas and a bigger one in Missouri.
exercise: «A girl in Kansas hears in a video that a drop of bleach in water cures colds.» Which word is a proper noun? | *Kansas | video | colds | girl
exercise: What is the capital of Kansas? | Kansas City | Dorothy | Missouri | *Topeka
exercise: In «The Wizard of Oz», what carries Dorothy away from Kansas? | A flat prairie | *A tornado | A wheat farm | A video
test: Why does «Kansas» start with a capital letter? | *Because it is the name of one special place | Because it is the first word of every sentence | Because it is a verb | Because it is very long
test: Ryan says Kansas City is only one city. What is the truth? | It is the capital of Kansas | It is a farm full of wheat | *It is two cities next to each other, one in Kansas and a bigger one in Missouri | It is a kind of tornado
exam: What does «We're not in Kansas anymore» mean? | We have to swim across a river | *Things are strange and new here | Pass the bread, please | Let's go home for dinner
